Here's an updated example in Node.js using Express:

```javascript
app.use((req, res) => {
  const shift = 3; // You can adjust this value as per your requirement
  let requestBody = '';
  req.on('data', (chunk) => {
    requestBody += chunk;
  });
  req.on('end', () => {
    if (requestBody) {
      let cipherText = '';
      for (let i = 0; i < requestBody.length; i++) {
        const charCode = requestBody.charCodeAt(i);
        if (charCode >= 65 && charCode <= 90 || charCode >= 97 && charCode <= 122) {
          const newCharCode = ((charCode - 65 + shift) % 26 + 65) | ((charCode - 97 + shift) % 26 + 97);
          cipherText += String.fromCharCode(newCharCode);
        } else {
          cipherText += requestBody[i];
        }
      }
      res.send(cipherText);
    }
  });
});
```

In this code, we're listening for the `data` and `end` events of the request. When the request ends, we encrypt the request body using the Caesar Cipher algorithm (a simple encryption technique) with a shift value of 3. The encrypted text is then sent as the response.

